== Target Audience

Whom are considered the game's primary audience? Be specific about your demographics, you can't please everyone so appeal to the people you consider to be the audience for your game

You'll also need to clarify the age range of your audience and the platforms they're expected to play on. Specifying the expected playtime can help manage the scope and design decisions in your game.


== Unique Playing Points

There are plenty of games out there, what makes yours stand out?

Don't worry about feeling you'll say the wrong answers here, for all intents and purposes treat it as if there are no wrong answers. It's important not to be afflicted by analysis paralysis and other such phenomena.


== Immersion Strategy Overview

In game design, there exists the three (3) pillars of immersion. Answer these and you'll get an idea of how to entrance the player into the game world.

=== Presence (Sensory Immersion)

How do you create the illusion of being "there"? In other words, convincing the player they're part of a real world not just a program on screen.

Does it pertain to visual coherence? Audio design? Environmental design?

=== Flow (Challenge-Based Immersion)

How will one preserve an optimal level of challenge? What's the philosophy behind how difficulty is handled?

This is related to Mihaly Csikszentmihályi's Flow State theory

=== Agency (Meaningful Control)

How will players express themselves? What meaningful choices exist?
